(window.FREE_Q = window.FREE_Q || {})["vii"] = {"qs": [["Investor Grievances and Arbitration", "An investor wants to complain online against a listed company and track the complaint. The platform is:", ["SCORES", "UPI", "e-voting", "MF Utility"], 0, "SCORES is SEBI's online grievance redress system for listed companies and intermediaries, with tracking of status and escalation."], ["Settlement Process", "A trade is executed on Wednesday. Thursday is a settlement holiday. Under T+1, settlement happens on:", ["Thursday", "Wednesday", "Friday", "Monday"], 2, "T+1 counts working days for settlement. With Thursday a settlement holiday, the next working day, Friday, is the settlement day."], ["Other Services Provided by Brokers", "Under Securities Lending and Borrowing (SLB), a lender of shares earns:", ["A lending fee", "The full market gain", "Voting rights only", "A guaranteed dividend"], 0, "The lender earns a lending fee while lending shares through the SLB platform. The borrower uses the shares, often to cover short sales or settlement needs."], ["Investor Grievances and Arbitration", "Under SEBI's ODR mechanism, a dispute is first attempted to be resolved through:", ["A civil court hearing", "Direct arbitration only", "A complaint to RBI", "Online conciliation"], 3, "The ODR process starts with online conciliation. If that fails, the matter can proceed to online arbitration through empanelled ODR institutions."], ["Introduction to Securities Broking Operations", "A contract note issued by a broker must contain:", ["The client's net worth", "Trade details and charges", "The client's tax computation", "The broker's balance sheet"], 1, "Contract notes show trade time, price, quantity, brokerage, taxes and other levies. They are legal records of the trade and must be issued within 24 hours."], ["Clearing Process", "A broker's clients buy 1,000 shares and sell 700 shares of the same stock in the same settlement. The broker's net securities obligation is to:", ["Deliver 300 shares", "Receive 300 shares", "Receive 1,700 shares", "Deliver 1,000 shares"], 1, "Netting offsets buys and sells: 1,000 bought − 700 sold = net receipt of 300 shares. Netting reduces the volume of securities and funds that must move."], ["Other Services Provided by Brokers", "Which service requires a broker to register separately with SEBI?", ["Order placement for cash market trades", "Portfolio management", "Issuing contract notes", "Providing a trading app"], 1, "Portfolio management requires separate SEBI registration as a portfolio manager. The other activities are part of normal broking operations."], ["Risk Management", "Upfront margin in the cash segment must be collected from clients:", ["Within seven days of the trade", "Only on loss-making trades", "At the end of the month", "Before trading"], 3, "SEBI requires brokers to collect VaR and ELM margins upfront from clients before trades are executed, reducing default risk."], ["Other Services Provided by Brokers", "In the Margin Trading Facility, the securities bought by the client are:", ["Transferred to the broker's own account", "Rematerialised", "Pledged in favour of the broker", "Kept with SEBI"], 2, "MTF positions are held in the client's account and pledged to the broker as collateral for the funding provided."], ["Other Services Provided by Brokers", "A broker offering demat services to clients must also be registered as a:", ["Merchant banker", "Depository Participant", "Mutual fund", "Credit rating agency"], 1, "Opening and maintaining demat accounts requires DP registration with SEBI through a depository."], ["Investor Grievances and Arbitration", "A client finds unauthorised trades in her account. Her first step should be to:", ["Ignore it if the amount involved is small", "Sell all holdings", "Raise it with the broker in writing", "Close her bank account"], 2, "Investors should promptly report unauthorised trades to the broker in writing; if not resolved, escalate to the exchange or SCORES and then ODR."], ["Clearing Process", "A trader buys and sells the same quantity of a stock on the same day in the cash segment. Under netting, the delivery obligation is:", ["Nil, only price difference settled", "Full delivery of both legs", "Delivery of the bought shares", "Settled next month"], 0, "Intraday buy and sell of equal quantity net to zero shares; only the money difference (profit or loss) is settled."], ["Clearing Process", "Why does netting reduce settlement risk?", ["It eliminates price risk for both buyers and sellers", "Fewer funds and securities need to move", "It guarantees profits", "It removes the need for margins"], 1, "By offsetting buys and sells, netting lowers the gross amount to be settled, reducing liquidity needs and the impact of any single failure."], ["Market Participants in the Securities Market", "Which of these is NOT a market infrastructure institution?", ["A stock exchange", "A merchant banker", "A clearing corporation", "A depository"], 1, "MIIs are stock exchanges, clearing corporations and depositories. Merchant bankers are intermediaries registered with SEBI."], ["Settlement Process", "Which statement about settlement of trades in the cash segment is correct?", ["Each trade settles separately on a gross basis", "Settlement depends on client consent", "Obligations are settled on a net basis", "Settlement is optional"], 2, "Clearing corporations net each member's buys and sells across trades in a settlement, and only net obligations are settled."], ["Other Services Provided by Brokers", "A broker's research report recommending stocks is covered by:", ["RBI's KYC Master Directions for banks", "IRDAI's guidelines", "SEBI Research Analyst Regulations", "The Depositories Act"], 2, "Research reports must comply with SEBI's Research Analyst Regulations, including disclosures on conflicts of interest and holdings."], ["Risk Management", "VaR margin is typically computed to cover potential losses on a position over:", ["The entire remaining life of the company", "Ten years of history", "Only the next minute", "A short period at a high confidence level"], 3, "VaR estimates the maximum likely loss over a short horizon (usually a day or two) at a high confidence level such as 99%."], ["Investor Grievances and Arbitration", "The Investor Protection Fund of a stock exchange pays claims when:", ["Share prices fall", "A broker is declared a defaulter", "A company skips a dividend", "An IPO is undersubscribed"], 1, "IPF compensates eligible investor claims against defaulting brokers up to specified limits; it does not cover investment losses due to market movement."], ["Clearing Process", "Clearing members' fund obligations are settled through:", ["Cash deposits at the exchange", "Cheques to SEBI", "The RTA's account", "Designated clearing banks"], 3, "Each clearing member maintains a settlement account with a clearing bank, which is debited or credited as instructed by the clearing corporation."], ["Market Participants in the Securities Market", "The intermediary that manages a public issue, prepares the offer document and conducts due diligence is the:", ["Registrar to the issue", "Merchant banker", "Stock broker", "Clearing bank"], 1, "Merchant bankers (book running lead managers) handle the issue process, due diligence and offer document. The registrar processes applications and allotments."], ["Settlement Process", "If a security cannot be bought in the auction after a short delivery, the trade is:", ["Closed out at a prescribed price", "Cancelled with no payment to either party", "Kept open indefinitely", "Settled by SEBI"], 0, "When an auction fails, the buyer is compensated through close-out at a price set by the clearing corporation's rules, usually higher than the trade price."], ["Introduction to Securities Broking Operations", "Which unit in a broking firm usually handles settlement of funds and securities with the clearing corporation?", ["Back office", "Front office", "Research desk", "Marketing team"], 0, "The back office handles post-trade work: settlement, reconciliation, client ledgers and statutory reporting. The front office takes orders."], ["Introduction to Securities Broking Operations", "Every order placed on the exchange must be tagged with the client's:", ["Bank branch code", "Unique Client Code", "ISIN", "Contract note number"], 1, "The UCC links each order to a registered client with KYC details, enabling exchange surveillance and client-level monitoring."], ["Introduction to Securities Broking Operations", "Funds received from clients must be:", ["Kept in client bank accounts, segregated", "Used for the broker's proprietary trades", "Deposited in the broker's own savings account", "Lent to other clients"], 0, "Client funds must be kept in designated client bank accounts and used only for that client's obligations, under SEBI's segregation rules."], ["Risk Management", "The Core SGF of a clearing corporation is used:", ["To meet settlement when a member defaults", "To pay dividends to investors of listed companies", "To fund IPOs", "To pay exchange salaries"], 0, "The Core Settlement Guarantee Fund guarantees completion of settlement if a clearing member defaults, after using the defaulter's own resources."], ["Introduction to the Securities Market", "Which of these trades on the derivatives segment of a stock exchange?", ["Treasury bills", "Bank fixed deposits", "Index futures", "Commercial paper"], 2, "Index futures and options are exchange-traded derivatives. T-bills and CPs are money market instruments, and FDs are bank deposits."], ["Clearing Process", "Institutional trades executed by a broker for a custodian's client become final only after:", ["The custodian confirms them", "The issuer approves them", "SEBI approves them", "The client pays tax"], 0, "Trades with a custodial participant code must be confirmed by the custodian; on confirmation, settlement responsibility moves to the custodian's clearing member."], ["Market Participants in the Securities Market", "An anchor investor in an IPO is:", ["A QIB allotted shares before the issue opens", "Any retail investor who bids on the first day of the issue", "The promoter of the company", "A stock exchange"], 0, "Anchor investors are QIBs who are allotted shares a day before the issue opens to the public, subject to lock-in, which helps signal institutional demand."], ["Market Participants in the Securities Market", "In a public issue, an individual investor applying for shares worth up to ₹2 lakh is classified as a:", ["Non-institutional investor", "Qualified institutional buyer", "Retail individual investor", "Anchor investor"], 2, "Individuals applying for up to ₹2 lakh are retail individual investors. Applications above ₹2 lakh by individuals fall in the non-institutional category."], ["Other Services Provided by Brokers", "A key risk for a client using MTF is that:", ["Brokerage is fully waived", "Dividends are lost", "Shares cannot be sold later", "Leverage magnifies losses"], 3, "Borrowed money magnifies gains and losses. A fall in prices can trigger margin calls and forced sale of pledged shares."], ["Introduction to Securities Broking Operations", "A client places a market order to buy 100 shares. The order will:", ["Execute only at the previous close", "Wait until a limit price is reached", "Execute at the best available price", "Be cancelled if not executed in 10 minutes"], 2, "A market order executes immediately against the best available sell orders, giving certainty of execution but not of price."], ["Market Participants in the Securities Market", "Which intermediary assigns ratings to debt instruments based on the issuer's ability to repay?", ["A credit rating agency", "A merchant banker", "A custodian", "A depository"], 0, "Credit rating agencies, registered with SEBI, assess the creditworthiness of issuers and their debt instruments. Ratings help investors judge default risk."], ["Introduction to Securities Broking Operations", "Before executing trades for a new client, a broker must ensure:", ["The client has traded elsewhere before", "The client has a minimum income of ₹10 lakh", "The client has bought an IPO", "KYC and client registration are complete"], 3, "KYC, PAN verification and client registration documentation are prerequisites for trading. No minimum income or prior trading history is required."], ["Introduction to the Securities Market", "Which of these is a money market instrument?", ["Equity share", "Ten-year corporate debenture", "Convertible preference share", "Commercial paper"], 3, "Commercial paper is an unsecured short-term instrument (up to one year) issued by companies. Shares and long-term debentures are capital market instruments."], ["Risk Management", "A broker collects less margin than required for a client's position. The consequence is that:", ["The trade is automatically cancelled", "The client gets a refund", "A penalty for short collection is levied", "SEBI pays the shortfall"], 2, "Clearing corporations levy penalties on short collection of margins, which brokers cannot pass on in a manner violating norms; the aim is to enforce upfront margin discipline."], ["Introduction to the Securities Market", "Interest rates in the overnight call money market are most directly influenced by:", ["Daily movement of the Nifty and Sensex indices", "RBI's liquidity operations and policy rate", "Company dividend announcements", "Gold prices"], 1, "Call money rates move within RBI's policy corridor and respond to RBI's liquidity operations, which add or absorb banking system funds."], ["Other Services Provided by Brokers", "A broker offering algorithmic trading to clients must:", ["Allow unlimited orders per second", "Avoid audits", "Hide the algos from the exchange", "Get exchange approval for the algos"], 3, "Algorithmic trading requires exchange approval, risk controls and audits so that algorithms do not disrupt markets or cause runaway orders."], ["Investor Grievances and Arbitration", "Four clients write to their broker. Whose grievance is a genuine service deficiency by the broker?", ["Loss on a stock bought on own decision", "The market fell after purchase", "Non-receipt of funds after a sale", "A company cut its dividend"], 2, "Failure to pay out sale proceeds is a service deficiency. Market losses on one's own decisions are not grounds for complaint."], ["Investor Grievances and Arbitration", "Which of these is NOT covered by the Investor Protection Fund?", ["Securities due from a defaulting broker", "Funds due from a defaulting broker", "Eligible claims after default", "Losses from speculative trades"], 3, "IPF covers eligible claims for funds and securities due from a defaulting broker. It does not compensate losses on trades made by the investor."], ["Market Participants in the Securities Market", "A stock broker's agent who deals with clients and is approved by the exchange is called a(n):", ["Sub-custodian", "Sponsor", "Trustee", "Authorised person"], 3, "Authorised persons act on behalf of a broker and must be approved by the exchange. Investors' contracts remain with the stock broker."], ["Introduction to the Securities Market", "Which regulator oversees banks' participation in the government securities and money markets?", ["SEBI", "IRDAI", "PFRDA", "RBI"], 3, "RBI regulates the government securities, money and forex markets and the banks that participate in them, while SEBI regulates stock exchanges and securities intermediaries."], ["Market Participants in the Securities Market", "A foreign pension fund investing in Indian listed shares would participate as a(n):", ["Authorised person", "Domestic institutional investor", "Foreign portfolio investor", "Retail individual investor"], 2, "Foreign institutional investors register with SEBI (through DDPs) as FPIs to invest in Indian listed securities."], ["Risk Management", "Concentration limits imposed by a broker on a single client mainly reduce:", ["Brokerage revenue earned from the client", "Exposure to one client or stock", "Number of trades", "Exchange fees"], 1, "Concentration limits prevent a large part of the broker's risk being tied to one client or one illiquid stock, which could cause big losses on default."], ["Settlement Process", "The cost of an auction resulting from a seller's short delivery is borne by:", ["The buyer", "The defaulting seller", "The exchange", "SEBI"], 1, "The seller who failed to deliver bears the auction price difference and penalties, discouraging short sales without delivery in the cash segment."], ["Risk Management", "A client sells 200 shares at ₹750. The day's closing price is ₹765. The mark-to-market loss is:", ["₹3,000", "₹1,500", "₹15,000", "No loss, since the sale is complete"], 0, "The short position loses when price rises: 200 × (765 − 750) = ₹3,000. MTM is computed against the closing price until settlement."], ["Settlement Process", "Funds pay-in for a clearing member must be made:", ["Any time within a week", "By the settlement-day pay-in time", "Only after pay-out", "On the trade date before market opens"], 1, "Clearing members must ensure funds are available in their clearing bank account by the pay-in deadline on settlement day so pay-out can proceed."], ["Introduction to the Securities Market", "A listed company raises fresh capital by allotting shares only to qualified institutional buyers. This is a:", ["Rights issue to all existing shareholders", "Qualified Institutions Placement", "Bonus issue", "Initial public offer"], 1, "A QIP lets listed companies raise money quickly from QIBs without a full public issue. A rights issue is offered to existing shareholders, and a bonus issue raises no money."], ["Introduction to Securities Broking Operations", "A client wants to buy only if the price falls to ₹420 or below. The client should place a:", ["Market buy order", "Stop-loss sell order at ₹420", "Limit buy order at ₹420", "IOC sell order"], 2, "A limit buy order executes only at the specified price or lower. A market order would buy at the current price, above ₹420."], ["Clearing Process", "Novation in clearing means that:", ["Trades are cancelled if prices move by more than the circuit limit", "Brokers settle directly with each other", "SEBI guarantees each trade", "The clearing corporation becomes counterparty to both sides"], 3, "With novation, the clearing corporation interposes itself between buyer and seller, so each party's counterparty risk is on the clearing corporation."], ["Settlement Process", "A client sells shares on Monday under T+1. The broker transfers the sale proceeds to the client:", ["After pay-out on Tuesday", "Before the sale on Monday", "Next month", "Only on request after a year"], 0, "Funds pay-out happens on settlement day (Tuesday); the broker then credits the client, subject to SEBI's timelines for releasing client funds."], ["Introduction to the Securities Market", "An offer of new shares by a company to its existing shareholders in proportion to their holdings is a:", ["Rights issue", "Private placement", "Preferential allotment to promoters", "Buyback offer"], 0, "Rights issues let existing shareholders subscribe in proportion to holdings, often at a discount; they can renounce rights entitlements if they do not wish to subscribe."], ["Other Services Provided by Brokers", "Mutual fund orders placed by a broker on an exchange platform are settled through:", ["The clearing corporation", "The AMC's sponsor", "The trustee", "RBI"], 0, "Exchange mutual fund platforms route orders to AMCs/RTAs, with funds and units settled through the clearing corporation."], ["Risk Management", "Peak margin requirements mean that brokers must ensure:", ["Margins are checked only at end of day", "Clients pay margin after settlement", "Margins cover intraday peak positions", "No margin applies to intraday trades"], 2, "Clearing corporations take multiple intraday snapshots and check collected margin against the peak requirement, so clients cannot take positions without adequate margin."], ["Investor Grievances and Arbitration", "Arbitration awards under the exchange or ODR mechanism are:", ["Merely advisory and can be ignored by the broker", "Binding, subject to legal challenge", "Valid only if SEBI signs", "Applicable only to brokers"], 1, "Arbitral awards are binding on parties, subject to challenge under the Arbitration and Conciliation Act. They provide a faster route than civil courts."], ["Risk Management", "A client's ledger shows an unpaid debit and the stock is falling. A broker's risk policy would typically allow it to:", ["Ignore it till the client pays", "Square off positions after notice", "Use another client's securities", "Charge interest only"], 1, "Brokers can liquidate positions as per their disclosed risk management policy to recover dues and limit further losses."], ["Market Participants in the Securities Market", "Which participant settles funds of clearing members with the clearing corporation?", ["Credit rating agency", "Authorised person", "Investment adviser", "Clearing bank"], 3, "Clearing banks maintain settlement accounts of clearing members and move funds for pay-in and pay-out as instructed by the clearing corporation."], ["Investor Grievances and Arbitration", "Exchanges maintain a mechanism to resolve complaints between investors and listed companies or brokers mainly because:", ["They supervise their members and listed companies", "They guarantee investment returns", "They are owned by investors", "They set income tax rates"], 0, "Exchanges have regulatory responsibilities over members and listed companies, so they handle investor complaints and can take action against them."], ["Introduction to the Securities Market", "A company already listed on the exchange makes a further public offer of new shares. This is called an:", ["FPO", "IPO", "OFS by promoters", "ESOP"], 0, "A follow-on public offer is a fresh issue by an already listed company. An IPO is the first public offer, and an offer for sale involves existing shareholders selling, not new shares."], ["Settlement Process", "Under the optional T+0 settlement cycle, trades are settled:", ["One day later", "Two days later", "At month end", "On the same day"], 3, "SEBI's optional T+0 (beta) framework lets a limited set of stocks settle on the trade day itself, alongside the regular T+1 cycle."], ["Settlement Process", "When a company declares a stock split, open stock futures positions are:", ["Closed out by the exchange at the last traded price", "Left unchanged", "Converted into options", "Adjusted in lot size and price"], 3, "Exchanges adjust derivative contracts for corporate actions like splits and bonuses, changing lot size and price so the position's value is preserved."], ["Introduction to Securities Broking Operations", "A client wants an order that stays active only until the end of the trading day if not executed. This is a:", ["Immediate or cancel order", "Good till cancelled order", "Market order", "Day order"], 3, "A day order is valid only for the trading day; unexecuted quantity is cancelled at the close. An IOC must execute immediately or be cancelled."], ["Clearing Process", "Which entity provides clearing corporations with the details of trades executed?", ["The depository", "The RTA", "The stock exchange", "The clearing bank"], 2, "Exchanges pass trade data to the clearing corporation, which computes obligations and coordinates with depositories and clearing banks for settlement."], ["Clearing Process", "The difference between clearing and settlement is that clearing:", ["Moves securities; settlement computes obligations", "Happens after settlement", "Determines obligations; settlement discharges them", "Is done by depositories only"], 2, "Clearing works out each member's net funds and securities obligations. Settlement is the actual pay-in and pay-out of those funds and securities."], ["Introduction to the Securities Market", "Price discovery in the secondary market means that:", ["SEBI fixes share prices daily", "Issuers set prices each morning", "Prices emerge from buy and sell orders", "Prices remain unchanged till results"], 2, "In the secondary market, interaction of many buyers and sellers determines the market price, reflecting available information."]]};
